{% note
   text="Systemd manages services and other background processes on modern Linux systems.\n\nAttackers can abuse systemd services or timers to execute commands automatically, including during system startup." /%}

## Systemd Persistence Locations

{% twoColumn
   left="Common locations:\n\n/etc/systemd/system/\n/usr/lib/systemd/system/\n/lib/systemd/system/\n\nCustom services under /etc/systemd/system/ deserve attention."
   right="Systemd timers can trigger services at scheduled intervals.\n\nUseful commands:\n\nsystemctl list-timers --all\nsystemctl list-unit-files --type=service" /%}

{% terminal
   title="systemd enumeration"
   commands=[{cmd: "systemctl list-unit-files --type=service"}, {cmd: "systemctl list-timers --all"}, {cmd: "systemctl --type=service --state=running"}, {cmd: "ls -la /etc/systemd/system/"}] /%}

{% finding
   severity="informational"
   text="Suspicious Services\n\nLook for:\n• Unknown services\n• Services pointing to unusual paths\n• Executables under /tmp or user-writable directories\n• Unexpected services running as root\n• Recently created or modified unit files\n• Suspicious ExecStart commands" /%}

{% warning
   text="Services Aren't Automatically Malicious\n\nLinux systems can contain many legitimate custom services.\nCheck the service description, executable path, owner, permissions, and whether the service is expected before treating it as suspicious." /%}

{% tip
   text="Follow ExecStart\n\nWhen you find an interesting service, inspect its ExecStart value first.\nThen follow that path and investigate the referenced script or executable." /%}

{% terminal
   title="inspect a service"
   commands=[{cmd: "systemctl cat <service-name>"}, {cmd: "systemctl show <service-name> -p ExecStart -p User"}] /%}

### Takeaway

When investigating Linux persistence, don't just list running services. Inspect how they start, what they execute, where the executable lives, and which user runs it.
